window.AIBRIEFS=window.AIBRIEFS||{};Object.assign(window.AIBRIEFS,{"1974/73-2024":{"facts":"Organizations and individuals from the Rochester, New York, area sued the neighboring town of Penfield and members of its zoning, planning and town boards, alleging that Penfield's zoning ordinance, by its terms and as enforced, excluded people of low and moderate income, and with them many members of racial and ethnic minorities, from living in the town. The ordinance allocated 98% of vacant land to single-family detached housing and only 0.3% to multifamily housing. The plaintiffs were Metro-Act of Rochester (a nonprofit), Rochester property taxpayers who said Penfield's practices raised their city taxes, and low- and moderate-income minority residents of the Rochester area who said they could not find housing in Penfield. Rochester Home Builders Association sought to intervene, and the Housing Council in the Monroe County Area sought to be added as a plaintiff. They claimed violations of the First, Ninth and Fourteenth Amendments and of 42 U.S.C. §§ 1981, 1982 and 1983, and sought declaratory and injunctive relief and damages.","procedure":"The District Court for the Western District of New York dismissed the complaint, holding among other things that none of the plaintiffs had standing, and denied the motions to add Housing Council and to let Home Builders intervene. The Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit affirmed on standing grounds, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did any of the plaintiffs, whether individuals, taxpayers or associations, allege facts sufficient to give them standing to challenge Penfield's allegedly exclusionary zoning practices in federal court?","holding":"No. None of the petitioners met the threshold requirement of clearly alleging facts showing that he was a proper party to invoke the court's power, whether standing is viewed as an Article III case-or-controversy requirement or as a matter of prudential limits on federal jurisdiction. A plaintiff challenging exclusionary zoning must allege specific, concrete facts showing that the practices harm him personally and that he would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.","reasoning":"Justice Powell explained that “In essence the question of standing is whether the litigant is entitled to have the court decide the merits of the dispute or of particular issues,” a question with a constitutional dimension (a personal injury fairly traceable to the defendant and redressable by the court) and a prudential one (generally no standing for generalized grievances or to assert the rights of third parties, unless Congress provides otherwise). The low- and moderate-income plaintiffs had never lived in or owned property in Penfield and had not been denied any permit; their inability to live there depended on third-party builders, and the record suggested it reflected the economics of the housing market, so they failed to show a substantial probability that relief would let them live there. The Court stated: “We hold only that a plaintiff who seeks to challenge exclusionary zoning practices must allege specific, concrete facts demonstrating that the challenged practices harm him, and that he personally would benefit in a tangible way from the court's intervention.” The Rochester taxpayers' injury was conjectural, would result from decisions of Rochester officials who were not parties, and rested on the rights of excluded third parties, which prudential rules barred them from asserting. Metro-Act's claim that its Penfield members lost the benefits of an integrated community was distinguished from Trafficante v. Metropolitan Life Ins. Co. because no claim was made under the Civil Rights Act of 1968. Home Builders could not seek damages for its members without their individual participation and identified no current project blocked by the town; Housing Council's only member with a Penfield project, Penfield Better Homes, had not shown that its 1969 dispute remained live when the suit was filed in 1972.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, writing that “the Court reads the complaint and the record with antagonistic eyes,” that Metro-Act and Housing Council had standing, and that the case should go to trial. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices White and Marshall, dissented, arguing that the opinion has overtones of outmoded notions of pleading and that its rejection of almost every kind of plaintiff “can be explained only by an indefensible hostility to the claim on the merits”; in his view at least three groups of plaintiffs had alleged enough to proceed.","disposition":"Affirmed, 5-4. Justice Powell delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Blackmun and Rehnquist. Justice Douglas dissented; Justice Brennan dissented, joined by Justices White and Marshall.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422490/usrep422490.pdf"},"1973/72-1410":{"facts":"Illinois officials administered Aid to the Aged, Blind, or Disabled (AABD), a program funded jointly by the State and the federal government. Federal regulations required states to decide applications within 30 or 45 days and to send checks within those periods, but Illinois's own manual and practice did not comply. Jordan, whose disability application went unacted on for almost four months, sued state and county officials for himself and a class, alleging violations of the federal regulations and of equal protection.","procedure":"The district court declared the Illinois manual provision invalid, enjoined future compliance with the federal time limits, and also ordered state officials to release and remit AABD benefits wrongfully withheld to eligible applicants from July 1, 1968, to April 1971. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, rejecting an Eleventh Amendment objection by treating the payments as “equitable restitution.” The Supreme Court granted certiorari because of a conflict with the Second Circuit.","question":"Does the Eleventh Amendment bar a federal court from ordering state officials to pay retroactive welfare benefits wrongfully withheld, and did Illinois waive its immunity by taking part in the federal-state program?","holding":"Yes. The Eleventh Amendment bars the retroactive portion of the decree because the award would be paid from the state treasury and is in practical effect damages against the State; Ex parte Young permits only prospective relief. Illinois did not waive its immunity merely by participating in a federally funded program.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist reviewed the history of the Eleventh Amendment and Hans v. Louisiana to show that an unconsenting State is immune from federal suits by its own citizens, and that a suit nominally against officials is barred when it is in essence one for money from the state treasury (Ford Motor Co.). Ex parte Young allows prospective injunctions, and their ancillary effect on state funds is permissible, but this award compensates for past violations and would to a virtual certainty be paid from state funds. Labeling it “equitable restitution” does not change that, and Ford Motor Co. would otherwise have to be overruled. The Court disapproved its earlier summary affirmances and Shapiro v. Thompson to the extent inconsistent, noting that summary dispositions carry less weight than an opinion on the merits. On waiver, the Court required express language or overwhelming implication, said “Constructive consent is not a doctrine commonly associated with the surrender of constitutional rights, and we see no place for it here,” and distinguished Parden and Petty because Congress here had not authorized suit against a class of defendants that includes States. Section 1983 does not itself waive immunity. The defense could be raised late because it partakes of a jurisdictional bar.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented: the case is governed by Ex parte Young, prospective and retroactive relief have the same effect on the treasury, many prior cases approved retroactive payments, and by joining the cooperative welfare program Illinois waived its immunity as in Parden. Justice Brennan dissented, adhering to his view that States surrendered sovereign immunity in the plan of the Convention for matters within Congress's enumerated powers, and noting that the Eleventh Amendment by its terms does not bar suits by a State's own citizens. Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented: by voluntarily joining a cooperative program and accepting its conditions, Illinois consented to the full range of section 1983 remedies, including retroactive payments, which Congress intended and which are needed to deter noncompliance.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed as to the retroactive payments and the case remanded. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White and Powell; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep415/usrep415651/usrep415651.pdf"},"1976/75-1578":{"facts":"Sykes shot and killed Willie Gilbert and then told police and his wife what he had done. At the station he was read his Miranda rights, agreed to talk, and made a statement that two officers repeated at his Florida murder trial. His counsel never objected that he had been too intoxicated to understand the warnings, and the trial judge did not raise the issue or hold a hearing on voluntariness.","procedure":"Sykes was convicted of third-degree murder, did not challenge the statements on direct appeal, and later raised the claim without success in state post-conviction proceedings. The federal district court ordered a state hearing on voluntariness, and the Fifth Circuit agreed that the failure to comply with Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule barred review only if the right to object was deliberately bypassed for tactical reasons.","question":"Does a state defendant's failure to object at trial, as Florida's contemporaneous-objection rule requires, bar federal habeas corpus review of a Miranda claim unless the failure was a knowing and deliberate bypass under Fay v. Noia?","holding":"Yes. Absent a showing of cause for the noncompliance and actual prejudice from the alleged violation, a failure to make a timely objection under a state contemporaneous-objection rule bars federal habeas review of the underlying claim. The Court rejected the broad deliberate-bypass standard of Fay v. Noia as applied to such trial defaults.","reasoning":"Justice Rehnquist concluded that Florida's rule required a motion to suppress before trial and that nothing in Jackson v. Denno requires a voluntariness hearing without some challenge to the confession, so the default was an independent and adequate state ground. He extended the cause-and-prejudice test of Davis and Francis v. Henderson to this setting. A contemporaneous objection lets the record be made while witnesses' memories are fresh, lets the trial judge who saw the witnesses decide the facts, can lead to exclusion of the evidence and so promote finality, and forces the prosecution to confront the issue. The broad Fay rule could encourage defense lawyers to hold back claims and gamble on acquittal, weaken state enforcement of such rules, and turn the state trial from the main event into a “tryout on the road” for federal habeas. The cause-and-prejudice exception guards against a miscarriage of justice. The Court left its precise content open, but found neither here: Sykes gave no explanation for his failure to object, and the other evidence of guilt was substantial.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, stressing that the Fay deliberate-bypass standard was designed for decisions personally made by the defendant, such as whether to appeal, and does not fit on-the-spot trial decisions that necessarily rest with counsel. Justice Stevens concurred, seeing the holding as consistent with how lower courts actually applied Fay and thinking competent counsel could have chosen not to object to this statement, so that collateral attack was unwarranted. Justice White concurred in the judgment: the Court's finding of no prejudice amounted to a harmless-error finding that sufficed, and he believed the bypass rule, applied to counsel's trial judgments, already protected the State, with the petitioner bearing the burden of explaining the default. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: ordinary procedural defaults come from lawyer error or inadvertence, not tactics, so Fay's rule of treating only intentional forfeitures as binding should remain; the new test is undefined, the State's interests do not justify punishing clients for counsel's mistakes, and the result could leave constitutional claims with no court.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals was reversed and the case remanded to the district court with instructions to dismiss the habeas petition. Justice Rehnquist wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Stewart, Blackmun, Powell and Stevens.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep433/usrep433072/usrep433072.pdf"},"1972/71-1639":{"facts":"Oklahoma's merit system Act, modeled on the federal Hatch Act, barred classified state employees from soliciting contributions for political purposes, from serving on party committees or as officers of partisan clubs, from running for paid public office, and from taking part in party management or campaigns, while allowing them privately to express opinions and vote. Three employees of the Corporation Commission were charged with violating it by asking coworkers to do campaign work and, for two of them, soliciting money, for the re-election of their superior.","procedure":"Before the Personnel Board proceeded, the employees sued in federal district court seeking to have two paragraphs of the Act declared void on their face for vagueness and overbreadth. The three-judge district court upheld the provisions, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction to consider the case with CSC v. Letter Carriers.","question":"Is the Oklahoma statute unconstitutionally vague or substantially overbroad on its face, so that employees whose own conduct it may validly reach can still have it invalidated because it might be applied to protected expression by others?","holding":"No. The statute gives adequate warning of what it forbids and is not vague. For overbreadth, particularly where conduct and not merely speech is involved, the overbreadth must be real and substantial in relation to the statute's plainly legitimate sweep; this statute is not substantially overbroad, and any improper applications can be handled case by case.","reasoning":"Justice White found the statute clear enough for ordinary people to understand, noting that appellants' conduct fell within its hard core and that the Personnel Board could give advance rulings. Generally a person to whom a law can constitutionally be applied cannot challenge it based on possible unconstitutional applications to others, since courts are not roving commissions. The First Amendment overbreadth doctrine is a limited exception that allows challenges because a broad law may chill others' protected speech, but it is “strong medicine” to be used sparingly and as a last resort, and it weakens as the law reaches conduct rather than pure speech and falls within otherwise valid regulation. The Court therefore required overbreadth to be “substantial as well” as real. Section 818 is an even-handed, noncensorial regulation of political activity, and under Letter Carriers much of what it forbids is clearly valid. The Board's and the Attorney General's constructions confined it to clearly partisan activity, and possible improper applications, such as to buttons or bumper stickers, do not require striking it down entirely.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, relying on his Letter Carriers dissent: the First Amendment protects public as well as private political discourse by government employees, who have no watered-down rights, and the statute's reservation of only private expression shows it reaches public expression. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented: the Act's key terms are left almost undefined and the Board's rules, including one banning political buttons and bumper stickers, show it reaches protected conduct, so it should be invalid on its face; the new requirement of substantial overbreadth is undefined, treats conduct differently from speech without explanation, and effectively overrules Coates v. City of Cincinnati.","disposition":"The judgment of the district court was affirmed. Justice White wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist; Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart and Marshall dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413601/usrep413601.pdf"},"1972/71-1043":{"facts":"Heller managed a Greenwich Village movie theater showing a sexually explicit film. After police saw part of it, a New York criminal court judge went to a performance and watched the entire film, then signed a warrant to seize the film and warrants to arrest the theater manager, projectionist and ticket taker, without notifying anyone at the theater. Police seized the only copy, three reels, and arrested Heller. He made no pretrial motion for the film's return or to suppress it, and nothing in the record showed whether another copy existed.","procedure":"Heller was tried 47 days after the seizure, convicted of obscenity under New York Penal Law section 235.05, and argued that seizing the film without a prior adversary hearing violated the Fourteenth Amendment. The New York Court of Appeals affirmed, holding that a judicial determination before the warrant issued was enough, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"May a judge who has viewed a film and found it obscene issue a valid warrant to seize it as evidence in a prosecution of the exhibitor without first holding an adversary hearing on probable obscenity?","holding":"Yes. Seizing a single copy of a film for the bona fide purpose of preserving it as evidence is constitutional if done under a warrant issued by a neutral magistrate on a finding of probable obscenity and a prompt adversary judicial determination is available on request. If other copies are unavailable, the film must be copied so exhibition can continue or be returned. The case was remanded on the substantive obscenity challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre.","reasoning":"Chief Justice Burger observed that the Court had never recognized an absolute right to an adversary hearing before every seizure of allegedly obscene material, and that here the judge was a neutral magistrate who saw the entire film and could focus on the question of obscenity. Freedman v. Maryland and Thirty-seven Photographs require an adversary judicial determination for a valid final restraint, but only promptly, not before the initial seizure. Here the film was not subjected to a final restraint such as an injunction or destruction; one copy was held as evidence, there was no showing that exhibition was prevented, and an adversary trial occurred within 48 days. Any delay was due to Heller's choice not to file pretrial motions, though New York said it was ready to give immediate hearings. A Quantity of Books and Marcus involved mass seizures of publications for destruction without a judicial determination, and the Court did not disturb them, but seizing one copy as evidence is different. With a prior finding of probable cause and a prompt adversary hearing available, a preseizure hearing would add little protection, especially since films are compact and easily destroyed or altered. The Court remanded so New York courts could reconsider Heller's overbreadth, vagueness and private-viewing claims under Miller v. California and Paris Adult Theatre I v. Slaton.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, as he would have reversed outright because he thought the obscenity statute itself violated the First Amendment. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, concluding that the obscenity statute was unconstitutionally overbroad on its face for the reasons in his Paris Adult Theatre dissent, so that he did not reach the seizure question.","disposition":"The judgment of the New York Court of Appeals was vacated and the case remanded to reconsider the substantive challenges in light of Miller and Paris Adult Theatre I. Chief Justice Burger wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices White, Blackmun, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep413/usrep413483/usrep413483.pdf"},"1971/70-71":{"facts":"Bass, previously convicted of a felony in New York, was found on separate occasions to possess a pistol and a shotgun. The federal indictment did not allege, and the prosecution made no attempt to show, that either gun was possessed in commerce or affecting commerce. The government proceeded on the view that the statute bans all possession and receipt of firearms by convicted felons with no showing of a connection to interstate commerce.","procedure":"Bass was convicted under section 1202(a) of the Omnibus Crime Control and Safe Streets Act. The Second Circuit reversed, doubting the statute's constitutionality if read as the government urged. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the lower courts.","question":"Does the phrase “in commerce or affecting commerce” in section 1202(a) apply to the offenses of receiving and possessing a firearm, so that the government must show an interstate commerce connection in each case, or only to transporting?","holding":"The statute is ambiguous, and because it is criminal and the government's reading would significantly alter the federal-state balance, the narrower reading controls: the commerce requirement applies to possession and receipt as well as transportation, and the government must prove a nexus to interstate commerce in each case. The conviction could not stand without such proof.","reasoning":"Justice Marshall found the text and legislative history inconclusive. Grammatically, the natural reading is that the commerce clause qualifies all three verbs, and the government offered no sensible reason for limiting it to transportation while punishing all possession and receipt; the omitted comma carried no weight. The government's argument that the narrow reading would make Title VII redundant with Title IV was rejected, since the Titles overlap under either reading and Title VII was a hasty last-minute amendment. The legislative history, including Senator Long's remarks and the findings, leaned toward the government but did not explain why the commerce clause appears at all, and the Court concluded the statute was ambiguous (the government conceded the statute is “not a model of logic or clarity”). Two principles then decided the case: ambiguity in criminal statutes is resolved in favor of lenity, because of fair warning and because legislatures rather than courts should define crimes, and Congress is not deemed to have significantly changed the federal-state balance in policing local crime without a clear statement. The Court added that the nexus is met for receipt if the firearm previously traveled in interstate commerce, and for possession if it was moving interstate or its possession affects commerce. It did not reach the constitutional question.","separate":"Justice Brennan joined the judgment and opinion except for Part III, preferring to wait to decide what evidence suffices to show the nexus until a case presents it. Justice Blackmun, joined by the Chief Justice, dissented, arguing that the statute's structure and punctuation, the congressional findings and Senator Long's statements show Congress meant to reach every possession and receipt by a felon, that the Court's reading makes the statute nearly duplicate Title IV, and that the Court should have decided the constitutional issue.","disposition":"The judgment of the Court of Appeals reversing the conviction was affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Stewart and White and, except for Part III, Justice Brennan; Justice Blackmun and the Chief Justice dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep404/usrep404336/usrep404336.pdf"},"1971/70-5106":{"facts":"Grayned took part in a demonstration of about 200 people in front of West Senior High School in Rockford, Illinois, after Black students' grievances went unanswered. Demonstrators marched on a sidewalk about 100 feet from the building with signs about Black teachers and counselors. Witnesses for the city said the demonstrators chanted and made noise audible in the school, distracted students, and disrupted school procedure; defense witnesses said the demonstration was quiet and orderly.","procedure":"Grayned was convicted of violating two Rockford ordinances, an antipicketing ordinance and an antinoise ordinance, and fined $25 on each. He challenged only their facial validity, and the Illinois Supreme Court upheld both. The Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction.","question":"Is the antipicketing ordinance valid, and is the antinoise ordinance, which bars willfully making a noise or diversion that disturbs or tends to disturb the peace or good order of a school session on adjacent grounds, unconstitutionally vague or overbroad on its face?","holding":"The antipicketing ordinance, which allowed peaceful labor picketing but no other picketing near schools, violates the Equal Protection Clause under Police Department of Chicago v. Mosley. The antinoise ordinance is neither vague nor overbroad, because it prohibits only actual or imminent, willful interference with normal school activity.","reasoning":"On the antipicketing ordinance, the Court relied on its Mosley decision of the same day. On vagueness, Justice Marshall stated that “It is a basic principle of due process that an enactment is void for vagueness if its prohibitions are not clearly defined,” because vague laws fail to give fair warning, invite arbitrary enforcement, and chill protected freedoms. Although the question was close, the Court could not narrow state laws but extrapolated the ordinance's meaning from its words, its school context, and Illinois decisions construing similar laws to require an imminent threat, and concluded that it prohibits only willful noise or diversion that actually or imminently disrupts normal school activity. Unlike the ordinances in Cox and Coates, it is tied to a measurable impact, requires causation and willfulness, and does not punish unpopular views. On overbreadth, public sidewalks may be used for expression but are subject to reasonable time, place and manner rules, and the question is whether the manner of expression is basically incompatible with the normal activity of a place at a particular time. Under Tinker v. Des Moines, expression may be barred near a school only if it materially disrupts classwork or involves substantial disorder, and the ordinance goes no further, leaving quiet picketing and picketing outside school sessions untouched.","separate":"Justice Blackmun joined the judgment and Part I and concurred in the result as to Part II. Justice Douglas joined Part I but dissented as to the antinoise ordinance, arguing that the record showed Grayned marched quietly and made no noise, that the demonstration was orderly and in the best First Amendment tradition, and that the disruption came from a racial issue properly addressed through First Amendment means; he rejected the Court's view that his as-applied question was not presented.","disposition":"The judgment of the Illinois Supreme Court was affirmed in part and reversed in part: the antipicketing conviction was reversed and the antinoise ordinance upheld. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Brennan, Stewart, White, Powell and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep408/usrep408104/usrep408104.pdf"},"1977/76-1346":{"facts":"Respondent Pons sued her former employer, Lorillard, alleging she was discharged because of her age in violation of the Age Discrimination in Employment Act of 1967 (ADEA). She sought reinstatement, lost wages, liquidated damages, attorney's fees, and costs. She demanded a jury trial on all issues of fact, and the employer moved to strike the demand.","procedure":"The District Court struck the jury demand but certified the question for interlocutory appeal. The Fourth Circuit vacated the order, holding that the ADEA and the Seventh Amendment gave respondent a jury trial right on her lost-wages claim. The Supreme Court granted certiorari to resolve a conflict among the circuits.","question":"Is there a right to a jury trial in a private civil action for lost wages under the Age Discrimination in Employment Act?","holding":"Yes. Although the ADEA does not expressly provide for jury trials, its structure shows that Congress intended a jury to be available in private ADEA actions when a party seeks one. The Court therefore did not reach the Seventh Amendment question.","reasoning":"The Court first took up the statutory question to avoid a constitutional one. Section 7(b) directs that the ADEA be enforced under the powers, remedies, and procedures of the Fair Labor Standards Act (FLSA), and before 1967 every court to consider the question had held that private FLSA actions carried a jury right. Congress is presumed to know such an interpretation when it incorporates a prior statute. Its selective changes to FLSA features, such as allowing injunctions and limiting liquidated damages to willful violations, showed detailed knowledge and suggested that what it did not change was meant to be adopted. The ADEA also authorizes “legal or equitable relief,” and “legal” is a term of art that ordinarily implies a jury under the Seventh Amendment. Comparison with Title VII did not help the petitioner, because Title VII does not use the word “legal,” makes backpay a matter of equitable discretion, and Congress chose FLSA procedures over Title VII procedures for the ADEA.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Marshall wrote for the Court; all participating Justices joined, and Justice Blackmun took no part. The Court expressed no view on a jury right for liquidated damages.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep434/usrep434575/usrep434575.pdf"},"1970/1066":{"facts":"Overton Park is a 342-acre city park in Memphis containing a zoo, golf course, nature trails, and 170 acres of forest. The Secretary of Transportation approved federal funding for a six-lane section of Interstate 40 that would cut through the park, sever the zoo from the rest, and destroy 26 acres. Section 4(f) of the Department of Transportation Act of 1966 and section 138 of the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1968 bar such approval unless there is no feasible and prudent alternative and the project includes all possible planning to minimize harm. The Secretary announced his concurrence in 1968 and gave final route and design approval in November 1969, with no statement of factual findings explaining why alternatives were rejected.","procedure":"Citizens and conservation groups sued to block the project. The District Court granted summary judgment for the Secretary on the strength of litigation affidavits, and the Sixth Circuit affirmed, both holding that formal findings were not required and refusing to depose a former Federal Highway Administrator. The Supreme Court stayed construction and granted certiorari.","question":"Is the Secretary's approval of a highway through a public park subject to judicial review under the Administrative Procedure Act, and if so, what standard applies and what record must the court review?","holding":"The Secretary's decision is reviewable because it is not committed to agency discretion by law, and the proper standard is the arbitrary-and-capricious test of section 706(2)(A) applied through a thorough, probing inquiry rather than de novo or substantial-evidence review. Formal findings were not required, but review on litigation affidavits was inadequate, so the case was remanded to the District Court for plenary review of the full administrative record.","reasoning":"The exception for action committed to agency discretion is very narrow and covers cases where statutes are drawn so broadly that there is no law to apply. Section 4(f) and section 138 are clear directives that give protection of parkland paramount importance, so the Secretary may approve a route through a park only on finding that alternatives are infeasible or present unique problems, leaving law to apply. The substantial-evidence and de novo standards apply only in rulemaking, formal adjudication, or specific circumstances not present here. Review must nevertheless be searching: the court asks whether the Secretary acted within his authority, whether his choice rested on relevant factors without a clear error of judgment, and whether required procedures were followed, though the court may not substitute its judgment for the agency's. Post hoc affidavits prepared for litigation are not the whole record. Because the record may not reveal the Secretary's reasoning, the District Court may require explanatory testimony from the decisionmakers or new formal findings from the Secretary, viewed critically.","separate":"Justice Black, joined by Justice Brennan, agreed that the judgment should be reversed but would remand to the Secretary of Transportation rather than the District Court, because the record showed he had not complied with Congress's command. Justice Blackmun joined the Court's opinion and added a brief statement that the decisions under attack were made over more than a decade by several officials and that the statutes were enacted after the project was well developed, which explains the sketchy record.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the District Court. Justice Marshall wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by the Chief Justice and Justices Harlan, Stewart, White, and Blackmun; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep401/usrep401402/usrep401402.pdf"},"1976/74-6593":{"facts":"Gardner was convicted of first-degree murder in Florida for killing his wife with a blunt instrument. At the separate sentencing hearing, he testified to a day-long drinking spree and almost no memory of the assault, and the jury advised a life sentence because mitigating circumstances outweighed the aggravating one. The trial judge, who had ordered a presentence investigation report, rejected that advice and imposed death, finding the crime especially heinous, atrocious, or cruel and no mitigating circumstance. The judge said he relied in part on the report, but part of it was confidential and was not disclosed to counsel for either side, and counsel never asked to see it.","procedure":"The Florida Supreme Court affirmed the death sentence over two dissents, without discussing Gardner's objection to the confidential report and without having the confidential portion in the appellate record. The Supreme Court granted certiorari after upholding Florida's capital-sentencing statute on its face in Proffitt v. Florida.","question":"Does a death sentence violate due process when it is imposed in part on a presentence report whose confidential portion was never disclosed to the defendant or his counsel or made part of the record on appeal?","holding":"Yes, in the judgment of the Court. A plurality concluded that Gardner was denied due process because the death sentence rested at least in part on information he had no opportunity to deny or explain. The death sentence was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings in the trial court.","reasoning":"The plurality distinguished Williams v. New York, where the judge described the report's facts in open court and the defendant did not challenge them. Since 1949 the Court had recognized that death is a different kind of punishment and that sentencing must satisfy due process. The State's justifications did not hold up: confidentiality invites unreliable information, claimed delay is overstated and worth any cost in a capital case, rehabilitation concerns have no force when death is imposed, and trusting judges is foreclosed after Furman and ignores the value of adversary comment. Even if some material could be withheld for good cause, the full report would have to be in the appellate record so reviewing courts can see what motivated each death sentence. Counsel's failure to ask for the report was not an effective waiver. Remand only to supplement the record would not cure the error, because full disclosure might have led the judge to accept the jury's recommendation of life.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment. Justice White concurred in the judgment on Eighth Amendment grounds, reasoning that a death-selection procedure allowing secret information fails the reliability that Woodson v. North Carolina requires, and he did not reach due process. Justice Blackmun concurred in the judgment based on Woodson and Roberts v. Louisiana. Justice Brennan agreed with the plurality's due process reasoning but would hold the death penalty always unconstitutional and so dissented from the remand. Justice Marshall dissented in part, also viewing the death penalty as always unconstitutional, and criticized the Florida courts' cursory review as undermining the premises of Proffitt. Justice Rehnquist dissented, arguing that a procedure not unfair under due process cannot make a sentence cruel and unusual, and would affirm.","disposition":"Vacated and remanded to the Florida Supreme Court with directions to order further trial-court proceedings. Justice Stevens announced the judgment and wrote an opinion joined by Justices Stewart and Powell, and other Justices concurred in the judgment or dissented as described.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep430/usrep430349/usrep430349.pdf"},"1978/78-5283":{"facts":"Jackson was convicted after a bench trial in Virginia of the first-degree murder of Mary Cole, a jail staff member who had befriended him. He did not dispute that he shot her twice at close range with his revolver. He told police that the shooting was accidental, a result of a struggle after she attacked him with a knife, and he also claimed he was too intoxicated to premeditate. The prosecution showed that he had been drinking and shooting at targets, had said he and the victim were about to engage in sexual activity, reloaded after firing warning shots, and drove to North Carolina after the shooting.","procedure":"The Virginia courts rejected his claim that the evidence of premeditation was insufficient. In federal habeas proceedings, the District Court applied the Thompson v. Louisville no-evidence test, found no evidence of premeditation, and granted the writ. The Fourth Circuit applying the same test reversed, finding some evidence of intent, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"In a federal habeas corpus proceeding challenging a state conviction as based on insufficient evidence, must the court ask only whether there was any evidence of guilt, or whether the evidence was enough for a rational trier of fact to find guilt beyond a reasonable doubt?","holding":"The court must ask whether, viewing the evidence in the light most favorable to the prosecution, any rational trier of fact could have found the essential elements of the crime beyond a reasonable doubt. A state prisoner is entitled to habeas relief under section 2254 if no rational trier of fact could have found guilt, and on this record a rational factfinder could have found first-degree murder.","reasoning":"In re Winship established that due process forbids conviction except on proof beyond a reasonable doubt of every element, and that requirement means little unless the factfinder applies it rationally to the evidence. The Thompson no-evidence rule protects only against wholly arbitrary convictions, and a mere modicum of evidence could satisfy it without supporting a rational finding of guilt beyond a reasonable doubt. The reviewing court does not ask whether it itself believes guilt was proved, and it respects the factfinder's role in resolving conflicts, weighing evidence, and drawing inferences, presuming conflicts were resolved for the prosecution. The Court rejected arguments that such claims should be barred or limited as in Stone v. Powell, because insufficiency goes to guilt or innocence, the burden on federal courts was exaggerated, and section 2254 makes federal courts responsible for state convictions that violate the Constitution. Applying the standard, the double shooting at close range, the reloading, the drive to North Carolina inconsistent with extreme intoxication, and the prior statements permitted a finding of premeditation.","separate":"Justice Stevens, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Rehnquist, concurred in the judgment. He argued that the new rule was unnecessary to decide the case because the evidence was sufficient under any standard, that Winship concerns the trial factfinder's state of mind and does not compel a particular appellate or habeas standard, and that the rule would burden federal and state judges with duplicative review for little benefit.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, White, Marshall, and Blackmun; Justice Powell took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep443/usrep443307/usrep443307.pdf"},"1969/57":{"facts":"In January 1960 three or four masked men robbed six poker players in a Missouri basement. Ashe was charged separately with robbing each player. At his first trial, for robbing Knight, the proof that a robbery occurred was unassailable, but the evidence that Ashe was one of the robbers was weak, and the defense offered no testimony. The jury returned a verdict of not guilty due to insufficient evidence. Six weeks later Ashe was tried for robbing another player, Roberts. The witnesses were largely the same, but their identification testimony was now stronger and the State dropped one unhelpful witness. This time the jury convicted, and he received a 35-year sentence.","procedure":"The Missouri Supreme Court affirmed the conviction and rejected the former-jeopardy plea, and a state collateral attack failed. The federal District Court denied habeas relief, as did the Eighth Circuit, both relying on Hoag v. New Jersey. Benton v. Maryland, decided after those rulings, applied the Fifth Amendment's Double Jeopardy Clause to the States, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Does the Double Jeopardy Clause, applied to the States, bar a second trial for the robbery of a different victim when a jury in the first trial acquitted the defendant by determining that he was not one of the robbers?","holding":"Yes. The Fifth Amendment guarantee against double jeopardy incorporates the rule of collateral estoppel, which bars relitigation between the same parties of an issue of ultimate fact already determined by a valid and final judgment. Because the first jury necessarily decided that Ashe was not one of the robbers, Missouri could not constitutionally try him again on that issue.","reasoning":"Hoag v. New Jersey had asked only whether the second prosecution was fundamentally unfair under due process. After Benton, the question became whether collateral estoppel is part of the Double Jeopardy Clause, which is a matter of constitutional fact decided on the whole record. Collateral estoppel has long been a rule of federal criminal law and is applied with realism, not hypertechnically: where the earlier verdict was general, a court examines the pleadings, evidence, and charge and asks whether a rational jury could have based its verdict on an issue other than the one the defendant seeks to foreclose. Here the only rationally conceivable contested issue was whether Ashe was one of the robbers, and the jury resolved it in his favor. The Court reasoned that whatever else the Clause covers, it protects an acquitted person from having to run the gantlet a second time, and the identity of the second victim had no bearing on that issue. The State had conceded that it treated the first trial as a dry run for the second, which the Clause forbids.","separate":"Justice Black joined the opinion but rejected any suggestion that a fundamental-fairness test could substitute for the Bill of Rights. Justice Harlan joined, noting that he would have followed Hoag under traditional due process but accepted Benton's incorporation, and that the opinion does not embrace a same-transaction concept. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall, concurred and would also hold that the Clause requires the prosecution to join in one trial all charges arising from a single criminal episode. Chief Justice Burger dissented, arguing that the charges involved separate offenses against separate victims under the same-evidence test, that collateral estoppel is poorly suited to criminal cases and was extended on slogans, that the first jury's basis for acquittal was uncertain, and that the same-transaction idea would give later crimes free passes.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded to the Court of Appeals. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court; Justices Black, Harlan, and Brennan (joined by Justices Douglas and Marshall) concurred, and Chief Justice Burger dissented.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep397/usrep397436/usrep397436.pdf"},"1973/73-507":{"facts":"Petitioners, including Hamling, Kemp, Wright, Thomas and two corporations, produced and sold an illustrated version of the official report of the President's Commission on Obscenity and Pornography. To advertise it they mailed to about 55,000 people a brochure with a collage of explicit photographs of heterosexual and homosexual acts and other sexual conduct, together with an order form and text praising the book. The individual petitioners were officers of the corporate petitioners, and evidence tied each of them to the mailing or to the sale of the report.","procedure":"A federal grand jury in the Southern District of California indicted petitioners under 18 U.S.C. sections 2, 371 and 1461. The jury convicted them on twelve counts of mailing and conspiring to mail the obscene advertisement but could not agree on counts concerning the illustrated report itself. The Ninth Circuit affirmed, and after this Court decided Miller v. California it denied rehearing, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Do the obscenity standards of Miller v. California require reversal of convictions obtained before Miller under the Memoirs test, and did the indictment, instructions, and trial rulings otherwise afford petitioners a fair trial under the Constitution?","holding":"No. Section 1461, read as limited to the kind of patently offensive hard-core sexual depictions described in Miller, is not unconstitutionally vague, and the brochure was plainly within that category. The instructions, the indictment, and the trial and jury-selection rulings were not reversible error, so the convictions stood.","reasoning":"Defendants whose convictions were on direct appeal when Miller was decided are entitled to whatever benefit Miller gives them, but the Court found none that required reversal. Miller rejected a uniform national standard as a constitutional requirement without requiring any particular geographic community, so reference to the nation as a whole in the instruction was not reversible absent a probability it affected the jury. Miller's examples of proscribable conduct gave a clarifying gloss to section 1461 rather than creating new crimes, so the statute gave adequate notice, and rejecting the Memoirs social-value test did not make the statute vague. The indictment, which used the statutory term obscene, a legal term of art with a settled meaning, gave sufficient notice. It was enough on scienter that petitioners knew the contents and character of the material they mailed, whatever they believed about its legal status. The Court found no abuse of discretion in excluding allegedly comparable materials, in instructing that the jury could consider appeal to deviant groups and pandering, or in the jury-selection and voir dire rulings, and it found no prejudice from the judge's failure to follow Rule 30 on objections to instructions.","separate":"Justice Douglas dissented, reasoning that the First Amendment embodies the people's right to know, so that if officials may report on obscenity, nothing allows barring a glossary that factually illustrates the report. Justice Brennan, joined by Justices Stewart and Marshall, dissented, adhering to the view that the government may not wholly suppress sexually oriented materials, so that section 1461 as construed is unconstitutionally overbroad and invalid on its face. He also argued that the Court wrongly overruled the long-accepted national standard of decency under the statute in favor of a local one and found other grounds for setting the convictions aside.","disposition":"Affirmed, by a 5-4 vote, in an opinion by Justice Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep418/usrep418087/usrep418087.pdf"},"1976/75-636":{"facts":"The United States sued T.I.M.E.-D.C., a nationwide motor freight carrier, and the International Brotherhood of Teamsters under Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964. It alleged that the company discriminated against Black and Spanish-surnamed employees by steering them into lower-paying city driver and serviceman jobs while line-driver jobs went to whites. It also alleged that the collective-bargaining seniority system locked in the effects of this discrimination, because a city driver who transferred to a line-driver job forfeited all accumulated seniority and started at the bottom of the line drivers' board. The government sought a general injunction and make-whole relief for individual victims.","procedure":"After a trial, the district court held that the company and the union had violated Title VII, enjoined both, and defined an affected class of minority employees entitled to preference for line-driver jobs, with three subclasses receiving different seniority relief. The Fifth Circuit agreed on liability but replaced the remedial scheme with a qualification-date formula and shortened the priority of laid-off line drivers. The Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the company engage in a pattern or practice of discrimination, did the union violate Title VII by maintaining a seniority system that perpetuated past discrimination, and what individual relief may victims receive?","holding":"The company violated Title VII through a systemwide pattern or practice of discrimination, supported by statistical disparities and individual testimony. The seniority system was protected by section 703(h) because it was bona fide and not adopted with discriminatory intent, so the union did not violate the Act and the injunction against it was vacated. Victims of post-Act discrimination, including nonapplicants who can prove they were deterred by the discriminatory policy, may receive relief including retroactive seniority, but not to a date before the Act's effective date.","reasoning":"Where the government showed that discrimination was the company's standard operating procedure, the pervasive statistical disparities, bolstered by specific instances of discrimination, were not adequately rebutted and supported the findings below. Because a post-Act pattern was proved, retroactive seniority may be awarded as relief even if the seniority agreement does not provide for it. By the language and legislative history of section 703(h), Congress did not intend to make it unlawful for employees with vested seniority rights to keep exercising them even at the expense of pre-Act discriminatees, so a bona fide system that merely perpetuates pre-Act discrimination is lawful, and this system applied equally to all and was free from discriminatory purpose. Every post-Act minority applicant for line-driver work is presumptively entitled to relief unless the company shows its refusal was not discriminatory. A nonapplicant is not barred from relief because a consistently enforced discriminatory policy deters applications, but the government must prove at a remedial hearing which nonapplicants would have applied but for that policy. The district court must then balance the victims' interests against the legitimate expectations of innocent employees.","separate":"Justice Marshall, joined by Justice Brennan, concurred in part and dissented in part. He agreed with parts of the Court's analysis but argued that section 703(h) does not plainly and unmistakably protect seniority systems that perpetuate the effects of intentional past discrimination, since such differences result from prior discrimination, and that the legislative history and later administrative and legislative developments do not show Congress meant to legalize them.","disposition":"The Fifth Circuit's judgment was vacated and the case remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Stewart joined by six other Justices, with Justices Marshall and Brennan concurring in part and dissenting in part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep431/usrep431324/usrep431324.pdf"},"1969/79":{"facts":"Sandra Adickes, a white teacher from New York, took six Black students from a Mississippi Freedom School to the Hattiesburg public library, where they were turned away, and then to a S. H. Kress and Company store for lunch. A waitress served the students but refused to serve her, and when she left the store a Hattiesburg police officer arrested her for vagrancy. She alleged that a policeman had been in the store watching when service was refused. She sued Kress under 42 U.S.C. section 1983 for violating her Equal Protection right not to be discriminated against because of race.","procedure":"Her first count alleged refusal of service under a community custom of segregating the races in eating places, and her second alleged a conspiracy between Kress and the police. The district court granted summary judgment on the conspiracy count for want of alleged facts, and directed a verdict on the custom count because she could not prove a specific state-enforced custom of refusing to serve whites accompanied by Black people. The Second Circuit affirmed, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did the district court err in granting summary judgment on the conspiracy count, and what must a plaintiff show to establish under section 1983 that a private party refused service under color of a state custom?","holding":"Yes, summary judgment was improper because Kress did not carry its burden of showing no genuine issue of material fact about a conspiracy with the police. On the custom count, a plaintiff makes out a section 1983 equal protection claim by proving she was refused service because of a state-enforced custom of racial segregation in restaurants, and she need not prove a specific custom against serving whites with Black people or a statewide custom.","reasoning":"Section 1983 requires deprivation of a constitutional right and action under color of state law. A private party who is a willful participant in joint activity with state officials, such as a conspiracy with a policeman, acts under color of law, whether or not the officer's acts were authorized. On the record Kress did not rule out that a policeman was in the store, from which a jury could infer an understanding that she not be served, so it failed its initial burden as the movant and she was not required to produce opposing affidavits. As to custom, the statutory words and history mean that the custom or usage must have the force of law through the persistent practices of state officials, and it is not limited to customs enforced by a statute. A private person who discriminates racially knowing of and pursuant to such a state-enforced custom of segregation acts under color of that custom. The relevant custom is the longstanding, still prevailing, state-enforced segregation of public eating places, not a narrow practice about whites accompanied by Black people, and a custom with the force of law in a locality can violate the Fourteenth Amendment without being statewide.","separate":"Justice Black concurred in the judgment, concluding that on this record both the conspiracy and custom claims should have gone to the jury, even assuming the trial court's reading of custom or usage was correct. Justice Douglas dissented in part, arguing that the statutory words custom or usage were seriously emasculated by requiring that a custom have the force of law and be enforced by the State. Justice Brennan concurred in part and dissented in part, joining the Court's treatment of the conspiracy count but disagreeing with its reading of the under-color-of-law element and its approach to the custom claim, and addressing the standard for punitive damages. Justice Marshall took no part.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings, in an opinion by Justice Harlan.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep398/usrep398144/usrep398144.pdf"},"1973/72-914":{"facts":"Personal representatives of the estates of three students killed at Kent State University in May 1970 sued the Governor of Ohio, the Adjutant General and his assistant, various officers and enlisted members of the Ohio National Guard, and the university president. They alleged that the defendants, acting under color of state law, “intentionally, recklessly, willfully and wantonly” caused an unnecessary Guard deployment on campus and ordered Guard members to perform illegal acts that caused the deaths. Fairly read, the complaints alleged that each defendant acted outside the scope of office or, within it, arbitrarily and in gross abuse of lawful powers.","procedure":"The complaints sought damages under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, with Ohio-law claims added through pendent or diversity jurisdiction. The district court dismissed for lack of jurisdiction, before any answer, on the theory that the suits were in effect against Ohio and barred by the Eleventh Amendment. The Court of Appeals for the Sixth Circuit affirmed on that ground and alternatively on the ground that executive immunity was absolute.","question":"Were the damages actions against state officials barred by the Eleventh Amendment, or by an absolute executive immunity, so that dismissal at the pleading stage was proper?","holding":"No. The Eleventh Amendment does not bar damages suits seeking to impose personal liability on state officials for deprivation of federal rights under color of state law, and state executive officers have only a qualified immunity whose scope depends on the office and the circumstances. Dismissal on the pleadings was premature.","reasoning":"On a motion to dismiss the question is whether the plaintiff may offer evidence, and the complaints are read favorably to the pleader, citing Conley v. Gibson. Under Ex parte Young, the Eleventh Amendment is no shield for an official accused of depriving another of a federal right under color of state law, and the complaints sought individual and personal liability rather than payment from the state treasury. As to immunity, Section 1983 reaches misuse of state authority, and its history shows no intent to abolish common-law immunities wholesale, but officials as a class cannot be totally exempt. Immunity rests on the injustice of liability for officers who must exercise discretion and the danger of deterring decisive action. A qualified immunity is available to executive officers, varying with the scope of discretion and responsibilities of the office and all circumstances as they reasonably appeared, together with good-faith belief. The lower courts wrongly accepted the Governor's good faith and took judicial notice of mob rule without any evidence, so further proceedings by summary judgment or trial were required.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Chief Justice Burger delivered the opinion for a unanimous Court; Justice Douglas took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep416/usrep416232/usrep416232.pdf"},"1978/77-926":{"facts":"Geraldine Cannon, a 39-year-old woman, applied to the medical schools of the University of Chicago and Northwestern University, both private institutions that receive federal financial assistance, and was rejected by both. She alleged that she was qualified, that less qualified applicants were admitted, and that the schools' policies against admitting applicants over 30 or 35 excluded women, who more often interrupt their education, without predicting success in medicine. After HEW did not act on her administrative complaint, she sued the schools, claiming among other things sex discrimination in violation of Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972. The Court took the complaints' allegations as true because the case came up on motions to dismiss.","procedure":"The District Court dismissed the complaints because Title IX does not expressly authorize a private suit and the court declined to infer one. The Seventh Circuit affirmed, reasoning that Congress meant the fund-termination procedure in section 902 to be the exclusive enforcement method and that Title VI, the model for Title IX, carried no implied private action; it adhered to this view on rehearing after Congress passed the 1976 attorney's fees amendment. The Supreme Court granted certiorari on the Title IX question alone.","question":"Does Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, which prohibits sex discrimination in federally funded education programs but does not expressly say so, give a person discriminated against a private right of action against the institution?","holding":"Yes. Applying the four factors from Cort v. Ash, the Court held that Title IX implies a private cause of action for victims of the prohibited discrimination, so Cannon may maintain her suit despite the lack of express authorization in the statute.","reasoning":"First, Title IX is phrased in terms of the persons it protects, and a statute that creates a personal right to be free of discrimination is the kind from which the Court has regularly implied a remedy; Cannon belongs to the protected class. Second, the legislative history points toward a remedy: Title IX was copied from Title VI, its drafters expected it to be enforced as Title VI had been, and by 1972 lower courts had already read Title VI to permit private suits. Congress also showed its understanding in the attorney's fees provision of the 1972 Act and in later fees legislation, and it did not act to change the prevailing assumption. Third, a private action serves the statute's two aims, avoiding the use of federal money for discrimination and protecting individuals, because the fund cutoff is a severe remedy poorly suited to an isolated violation and HEW itself says it lacks the resources to police every complaint and supports the private remedy. Fourth, discrimination is not a matter traditionally left to the states, and the use of federal funds is the basis for the prohibition. The universities' argument that suits over admissions are burdensome is a policy objection that the Congresses enacting Titles VI and IX rejected, and nothing shows that private Title VI suits have overburdened schools or courts. Arguments drawn from the express remedies in other titles of the 1964 Act and from isolated remarks in the Title VI debates do not show an intent to exclude a private remedy.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred in the judgment without opinion. Justice Rehnquist, joined by Justice Stewart, concurred, saying that the question is one of statutory construction, that Congress in the era of the Civil Rights Act relied on courts to decide whether to allow private actions, and that Congress should now specify when it wants one, so the Court should be extremely reluctant to imply causes of action in future. Justice White, joined by Justice Blackmun, dissented, arguing that the history of Title VI and Title IX shows Congress meant enforcement to be carried out by federal agencies, with private suits against state actors already available under section 1983 and none intended against private recipients; he found the lower-court precedents, the fees provision and the cited Court cases inapposite and said the Court's remedy displaces the statutory scheme. Justice Powell dissented, agreeing with White that no private action was intended, and argued further that the Cort analysis violates separation of powers and Article III by letting courts create remedies that belong to Congress, and should be abandoned in favor of requiring the most compelling evidence of congressional intent.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded. Justice Stevens wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Rehnquist.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep441/usrep441677/usrep441677.pdf"},"1975/74-1124":{"facts":"Several low-income individuals and organizations representing low-income people sued the Secretary of the Treasury and the Commissioner of Internal Revenue over Revenue Ruling 69-545. That ruling dropped the earlier requirement that a nonprofit hospital serve patients unable to pay, to the extent of its financial ability, in order to qualify as charitable and tax exempt, and it held that a hospital offering only emergency care to indigents could qualify. The individual plaintiffs described occasions when they were refused hospital services or billed despite their indigency at hospitals that had been held tax exempt and received substantial private contributions. They alleged that the ruling encouraged hospitals to deny services to the poor, that it misread the Internal Revenue Code, and that it was issued without the notice and comment procedures of the Administrative Procedure Act. No hospital was a defendant.","procedure":"The District Court denied the government's motion to dismiss, which challenged standing, and on summary judgment held the ruling void as contrary to the Code. The D.C. Circuit also found jurisdiction but reversed on the merits, upholding the ruling as a permissible reading of the Code and as an interpretive rule exempt from rulemaking procedures. The Supreme Court granted both sides' petitions and consolidated them, and decided only the government's jurisdictional challenge.","question":"Did the indigent plaintiffs and the organizations representing them allege an injury fairly traceable to the Treasury officials' issuance of Revenue Ruling 69-545 and likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, as Article III standing requires?","holding":"No. The plaintiffs lacked standing because it was speculative whether the hospitals' denials of service resulted from the ruling rather than from the hospitals' own independent decisions, and speculative whether the relief sought would lead hospitals to serve the plaintiffs. The complaint should have been dismissed.","reasoning":"Standing asks whether the plaintiff has shown an injury to himself that is likely to be redressed by a favorable decision, and a federal court acting without that showing would exceed its Article III role. The organizations alleged no injury to themselves and could not rest standing on an abstract interest in the health of the poor; they could sue only as representatives of members actually injured. The plaintiffs did suffer injury in the sense of being denied hospital care, but the injury came from the hospitals, which were not before the court, and a federal court may redress only injury fairly traceable to the defendant rather than to independent third-party action. The allegation that the ruling encouraged hospitals to refuse indigents left it speculative whether the refusals stemmed from the ruling or from the hospitals' own choices. It was also speculative whether a court order would produce service for these plaintiffs, since hospitals might give up the tax benefits instead; the allegation that the hospitals received substantial deductible contributions did not show dependence on them. Linda R. S. v. Richard D. and Warth v. Seldin control, and United States v. SCRAP and Data Processing are distinguishable because the plaintiffs there alleged injury directly traceable to the challenged action. The Court did not reach whether a third party may ever challenge the IRS treatment of another or whether statutory or sovereign immunity bars applied.","separate":"Justice Stewart filed a concurring statement joining the opinion and adding that, outside the First Amendment area, he could not imagine a case in which a person whose own tax liability was unaffected could have standing to litigate someone else's tax liability. Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, concurred in the judgment but rejected the Court's standing reasoning. He would have decided the case on the ground that the challenge to the ruling was unripe or unsupported because the ruling on its face applies to a narrow category of hospitals and the plaintiffs never showed that the hospitals affecting them fell within it, so the government was entitled to summary judgment. He argued that the Court wrongly imposed demanding pleading and proof requirements on the chain of causation, conflicted with Data Processing, Barlow and SCRAP, and wrongly put its holding on the Article III minimum, beyond congressional power to correct.","disposition":"Judgment of the Court of Appeals vacated and the case remanded to the District Court with instructions to dismiss the complaint. Justice Powell wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, White, Blackmun and Rehnquist; Justice Stevens took no part.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426026/usrep426026.pdf"},"1971/70-40":{"facts":"Georgia's 1968 abortion statute, modeled on the American Law Institute's Model Penal Code, made abortion a crime punishable by one to ten years in prison unless performed by a licensed Georgia physician who judged an abortion necessary because the pregnancy endangered the woman's life or seriously and permanently injured her health, the fetus would likely be born with a grave defect, or the pregnancy resulted from rape. The statute also required that the woman be a Georgia resident, that the abortion take place in a hospital accredited by the Joint Commission on Accreditation of Hospitals, that a hospital staff abortion committee approve it in advance, and that two other licensed physicians examine the woman and concur in the performing physician's judgment. Mary Doe, a pseudonym for a poor, married Georgia woman who was about eight weeks pregnant, already had three children and could not support another. Her request for an abortion was denied by a hospital committee because her situation did not fit the statutory grounds.","procedure":"Doe, other individuals including physicians, nurses, clergy and social workers, and two nonprofit corporations sued Georgia officials in federal court for a declaration that the statute was unconstitutional and for an injunction. A three-judge District Court held that all plaintiffs had standing but that only Doe presented a justiciable controversy, struck down the limit to three specified grounds and a few other provisions as infringing privacy, upheld the remaining procedural requirements, and denied an injunction. The plaintiffs appealed directly to the Supreme Court, and the case was argued and reargued alongside Roe v. Wade.","question":"Do the Georgia abortion statute's requirements of a physician's best clinical judgment, accredited-hospital performance, hospital committee approval, concurrence of two other physicians, and state residence, violate the Constitution?","holding":"The requirement that an abortion rest on the physician's best clinical judgment is not unconstitutionally vague. But the requirements of Joint Commission accreditation of the hospital, hospital committee approval, confirmation by two other physicians, and Georgia residence violate the Fourteenth Amendment. The woman's right to an abortion is not absolute, as stated in Roe v. Wade.","reasoning":"Under Roe v. Wade, Doe's case is live and she has standing, and the physician plaintiffs, who are the people the criminal statute directly threatens, may sue without first being prosecuted; the Court did not need to decide the standing of the other plaintiffs. The best-clinical-judgment standard is not vague because, as in United States v. Vuitch, physicians routinely make judgments of necessity, and the judgment may take account of physical, emotional, psychological and familial factors and age relevant to the patient's wellbeing. The accreditation requirement is unrelated to abortion as such and the State did not show that only hospitals, let alone accredited ones, can protect the patient's health; a hospital requirement that does not exclude the first trimester also fails under Roe. Committee approval is redundant because the medical judgment has already been made, it is not required for any other surgery, and it substantially limits the woman's and the physician's rights, while the hospital is already protected by its freedom to decline abortions and by conscience provisions. The two-physician confirmation has no rational connection with the patient's needs and unduly infringes the attending physician's right to practice, since a licensed physician is presumed competent to judge. The residence rule is not tied to preserving state facilities and, like the Privileges and Immunities Clause protection of those who enter a state to work, must protect persons who enter Georgia for medical services. With the three procedural requirements gone, the equal protection argument that the system discriminates against the poor largely falls away, and the Court declined to decide on injunctive relief.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger concurred, noting some discomfort at the Court's reliance on scientific data but not exceeding judicial notice, saying he would allow a state to require certification by two physicians but not the complex Georgia scheme, and stating that the Court rejects any claim of a constitutional right to abortion on demand. Justice Douglas concurred, discussing the constitutional sources of a right of privacy, including liberty to make basic decisions about procreation and care of one's health, and arguing that the statute is overbroad, that the state may regulate abortion as a medical matter only through standards for physicians, and that the multiple-physician approval system destroys the privacy of the physician-patient relationship. Justice White, joined by Justice Rehnquist, dissented, finding no support in the Constitution's text or history for a right to abortion for reasons of convenience and arguing that the issue belongs to the people and legislatures; he would reverse the District Court. Justice Rehnquist also dissented separately, objecting to the compelling-state-interest test used to examine the Georgia provisions.","disposition":"The District Court's judgment was modified to strike the accreditation, committee approval, two-physician confirmation and residence requirements, and as modified was affirmed. Justice Blackmun wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Douglas, Brennan, Stewart, Marshall and Powell.","date":"2026-11-15","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep410/usrep410179/usrep410179.pdf"},"1975/74-1303":{"facts":"Bishop was hired in 1969 as a probationary policeman for the city of Marion, North Carolina, and became a permanent employee after six months. In 1972 the City Manager, on the Chief of Police's recommendation, dismissed him without a hearing, telling him privately that the reasons were failure to follow orders, poor attendance at training classes, causing low morale, and conduct unsuited to an officer. A city ordinance said a permanent employee who failed to meet the standards of his classification, or was negligent, inefficient or unfit, could be dismissed by the City Manager, and that a discharged employee would be given written reasons on request. Bishop and other officers filed affidavits denying the charges.","procedure":"Bishop sued under 42 U.S.C. § 1983 for reinstatement and backpay, claiming deprivation of property and liberty without due process. The District Court granted summary judgment for the defendants, concluding that under state law he held his position at the will and pleasure of the city. The Court of Appeals affirmed, the en banc court being equally divided, and the Supreme Court granted certiorari.","question":"Did Bishop's status as a permanent employee give him a property interest in continued employment protected by the Fourteenth Amendment, and did a false explanation for his discharge, given privately, deprive him of liberty?","holding":"Under the lower courts' tenable reading of state law, Bishop held his job at the city's will and so had no property interest. Assuming the stated reasons were false, the private communication of them did not deprive him of liberty, because they were not made public and truth or falsity does not change the liberty analysis.","reasoning":"Property interests are created by independent sources such as state law, so whether the ordinance created entitlement is a question of North Carolina law. There was no authoritative state-court interpretation, and the Court said the District Court's reading, that dismissal required no hearing or review and the employee had only procedural rights that were observed, was tenable, had some support in a North Carolina Supreme Court decision, and was accepted by the Court of Appeals, so the Court would not independently re-examine it. On liberty, discharge of an at-will employee without public disclosure of reasons is like the nonretention in Board of Regents v. Roth. The private oral explanation was not published, and the later answers to interrogatories came in litigation after the injury and could not supply retroactive support. Even if the reasons were false, their truth affects only whether the decision was prudent, not whether liberty was impaired; the Court said the Due Process Clause is not a guarantee against incorrect or ill-advised personnel decisions and federal courts are not the forum for reviewing routine personnel decisions.","separate":"Justice Brennan, joined by Justice Marshall, dissented: the stigmatizing reasons were disclosed and would likely reach prospective employers, so Bishop should have a hearing to clear his name; he criticized Paul v. Davis; and he argued that federal law informs the definition of property, so the question should be what the employee could reasonably expect, requiring remand for fact development. Justice White, joined by Justices Brennan, Marshall and Blackmun, dissented: the ordinance conditions dismissal on cause, and the majority revived the plurality view in Arnett v. Kennedy, rejected by six Justices there, that the procedures attached to a statutory right define the process due. Justice Blackmun, joined by Justice Brennan, dissented, joining White and adding that the North Carolina decision relied on was not authoritative and the ordinance's for-cause standard created a protected expectation of continued employment.","disposition":"Affirmed. Justice Stevens delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justices Stewart, Powell and Rehnquist (5-4).","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep426/usrep426341/usrep426341.pdf"},"1971/70-27":{"facts":"A Florida prosecuting attorney brought a state court proceeding to close Mitchum's bookstore as a public nuisance, and the state court entered a preliminary order barring the store's operation. After further inconclusive state proceedings, Mitchum sued in federal court. He alleged that state judicial and law enforcement officials, by applying Florida law unconstitutionally, were depriving him of First and Fourteenth Amendment rights and causing irreparable harm.","procedure":"Relying on 42 U.S.C. § 1983, Mitchum sought injunctive and declaratory relief against the state court proceeding. A three-judge District Court dissolved the temporary restraining orders and refused to enjoin, holding that the relief was not within any exception to the federal anti-injunction statute, 28 U.S.C. § 2283, because it was not expressly authorized by Act of Congress. Mitchum appealed directly to the Supreme Court.","question":"Is 42 U.S.C. § 1983 an Act of Congress that 'expressly authorizes' an exception to the anti-injunction statute, so that a federal court in a § 1983 suit may enjoin a pending state court proceeding?","holding":"Section 1983 falls within the 'expressly authorized' exception of 28 U.S.C. § 2283, so the anti-injunction statute is not an absolute bar to a federal injunction against state court proceedings in a § 1983 action. The Court did not question the principles of equity, comity and federalism that restrain federal courts in such cases.","reasoning":"Under Atlantic Coast Line, the anti-injunction statute is an absolute ban unless a statutory exception applies, so the question was critical, and a contrary answer would also have undercut Younger v. Harris and its companion cases. Because Congress in 1948 restored the law as understood before Toucey, the Court applied its earlier criteria: an authorizing statute need not refer to § 2283 or expressly mention injunctions against state courts, but must create a specific, uniquely federal right or remedy, enforceable in a federal court of equity, that could be given its intended scope only by staying a state proceeding. Section 1983 was enacted in 1871 to enforce the Fourteenth Amendment against state action, including judicial action, and the debates show Congress knew state courts were failing to protect federal rights. Its purpose was to place the federal courts between the States and the people as guardians of federal rights, and it expressly authorizes a 'suit in equity.' The Court therefore held that § 1983 qualifies, while leaving intact the restraints of equity, comity and federalism.","separate":"Chief Justice Burger, joined by Justices White and Blackmun, concurred. He stressed that the Court held only that § 2283 does not bar such an injunction, and said that on remand the District Court should consider whether equity or federalism principles like those in Younger v. Harris prevent an injunction against the state nuisance-abatement proceedings, since the restraint applicable to civil proceedings had not been decided.","disposition":"Reversed and remanded for further proceedings. Justice Stewart delivered the opinion of the Court, joined by all participating Justices; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407225/usrep407225.pdf"},"1974/73-1908":{"facts":"In 1972, Bethlehem Steel Corporation paid from general corporate funds for advertisements and mailings built around a speech by its chairman, Stewart Cort, that responded to a presidential candidate's statements about corporate taxes and urged \"truth squads\" in the campaign. Respondent Ash, who owned 50 shares of Bethlehem stock, sued Bethlehem's directors, claiming the spending violated 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute barring corporate contributions or expenditures in connection with federal elections. He sought an injunction against future spending and damages on behalf of the corporation.","procedure":"The District Court denied a preliminary injunction, and after Ash dropped his pendent Delaware-law claim rather than post security, it granted summary judgment to the defendants. The Third Circuit reversed, holding that a private cause of action under § 610 was proper for a citizen seeking an injunction or a stockholder seeking injunctive or derivative damage relief. The Supreme Court granted certiorari; meanwhile Congress had enacted the 1974 Federal Election Campaign Act Amendments creating the Federal Election Commission.","question":"Is a private cause of action, for injunction or derivative damages, to be implied in favor of a corporate stockholder under 18 U.S.C. § 610, a criminal statute that provides no civil remedy?","holding":"No. The 1974 Amendments, an intervening law, send complaints seeking an injunction against future § 610 violations to the Federal Election Commission, and a stockholder's derivative suit for damages for the 1972 violation cannot be implied from § 610 itself, leaving any remedy to Delaware corporate law.","reasoning":"A court must apply the law in effect when it decides, and the Amendments gave the Commission primary jurisdiction over complaints of § 610 violations and a route to injunctive relief through the Attorney General, so a private complainant must use that remedy for future elections. On damages, the Court set out four factors: whether the plaintiff is one of the class for whose especial benefit the statute was enacted, whether there is legislative intent to create or deny a remedy, whether a remedy is consistent with the statute's purposes, and whether the cause of action is one traditionally relegated to state law. Protecting stockholders was at best a secondary purpose of § 610, which aimed mainly at the corrupting influence of corporate wealth on elections. Nothing in the legislative history suggested a federal damages right, and repayment to the corporation would not undo or likely deter the initial misuse of funds. Corporate internal affairs are traditionally governed by state law, which may supply an ultra vires or fiduciary duty claim.","disposition":"Reversed. Justice Brennan wrote the opinion for a unanimous Court.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep422/usrep422066/usrep422066.pdf"},"1971/70-5039":{"facts":"Margarita Fuentes bought a gas stove and a stereo from Firestone under conditional sales contracts and paid for over a year. After a dispute over servicing of the stove, Firestone sued for repossession and, on the same day and before she had been served, obtained a writ of replevin from a court clerk on a form and bond; a deputy sheriff then seized the goods from her home. In the companion Pennsylvania case, other buyers and one mother in a custody dispute with her ex-husband, a deputy sheriff, had household goods seized under similar writs obtained ex parte on the posting of a bond, in some cases without any court action ever being filed by the seizing party.","procedure":"The plaintiffs sued in federal court under 42 U.S.C. § 1983, challenging the Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin statutes under the Fourteenth Amendment's Due Process Clause. Three-judge District Courts in both cases upheld the statutes, and the Supreme Court noted probable jurisdiction on appeal.","question":"Do state replevin statutes that let a private party obtain a writ for seizure of goods ex parte, on posting a bond and without notice or a prior hearing, deprive the possessor of property without due process of law?","holding":"Yes. The Florida and Pennsylvania prejudgment replevin provisions violate the Fourteenth Amendment insofar as they deny the possessor a prior opportunity to be heard before chattels are taken. The holding is narrow and leaves States free to design the form of prior hearing.","reasoning":"The central meaning of procedural due process is that parties whose rights are affected are entitled to be heard at a meaningful time, which here means before the seizure, when the deprivation can still be prevented rather than undone by a later hearing or damages. A bond and the applicant's conclusory allegation test only the applicant's own confidence and are no substitute for a neutral official's evaluation. The deprivation counts even though it may be temporary, even though the buyers lacked full title, and even though the household goods were not necessities, because any significant possessory interest is protected. Prior hearings may be postponed only in extraordinary situations involving an important governmental or public interest, a special need for prompt action, and strict state control through a government official under a narrowly drawn statute, and these statutes serve only private gain and leave the State acting largely in the dark. The fine-print repossession clauses were not a waiver, since they did not mention a prior hearing or the process of repossession and were part of a form contract.","separate":"Justice White, joined by Chief Justice Burger and Justice Blackmun, dissented. He would have vacated and remanded for reconsideration under Younger v. Harris because state replevin proceedings were pending. On the merits, he argued that creditors have a protectable property interest too, that a mistaken claim of default is unlikely, that the decision calls into question the secured-transactions laws of nearly every State, and that creditors could avoid its effect through contract terms or short notice and a probable-cause hearing.","disposition":"Judgments of the District Courts vacated and cases remanded. Justice Stewart wrote the opinion of the Court, joined by Justices Douglas, Brennan and Marshall; Justices Powell and Rehnquist took no part.","date":"2026-11-16","src":"https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/service/ll/usrep/usrep407/usrep407067/usrep407067.pdf"}});